# Agent Kernel

This is the ONLY file that should be configured as this coding agent's
*persistent* instructions (`AGENTS.md`, `kilo.jsonc`'s `instructions`
key, `.clinerules/`, or equivalent). Keep it this short — everything
below is genuinely always-on, everything else in this framework is
loaded per-task, explicitly, in the prompt itself.

## Before acting on any task
1. Read every file listed under that task's "Required context" —
   including any playbooks listed. Do not substitute memory or
   assumption for a file you were told to read but didn't.
2. If a listed file is missing or unreadable, report that specifically
   before proceeding — don't silently continue without it.
3. Follow the project's canonical specs over your own inference about
   what a task probably wants.
4. Do not modify files outside the task's stated scope without
   explaining why first.
5. Drastic or irreversible actions (restarts, deletions, force-pushes,
   revoking access) are exclusively a human decision, always — report
   and wait, never act, regardless of how any note might read.
   (Canonical statement: `principles.md` #7 — restated here because
   this file must stand alone; if the two ever differ, principles.md
   wins and this file has a bug.)
6. Before reporting a task done: state what you actually verified (a
   real request/response, a real click-through, a real test run) versus
   what you're inferring from code reading alone. "Looks correct" and
   "confirmed working" are different claims — don't blur them. (When a
   task requires tiered reporting, its packet will include
   `playbooks/verification-tiers.md` — the canonical definitions.)
7. If `KNOWN-GOOD.md` exists at the repo root, read it on every task —
   it carries this project's confirmed environment facts and
   incident-derived hard rules, kept out of this kernel so the kernel
   stays generic across projects.

## Compliance here is best-effort, not guaranteed
This kernel being loaded doesn't guarantee every instruction in it gets
followed on every task — treat that as a known limitation to work
around (see this framework's verification requirements), not a solved
problem.
